      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The leaves of <i>Mimosa pudica</i> plants are made of a number of structures known as pinnae. The pinnae fold when the leaf is touched. This closes the leaf.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 8
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Fig. 4.1 shows an open leaf of <i>M. pudica</i> before it is touched. Fig. 4.2 shows the same leaf that has closed after being touched.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 8
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "figure",
        "id": "fig4.1_4.2",
        "caption": "<b>Fig. 4.1　　Fig. 4.2</b>",
        "src": "figures/q4_fig4_1_4_2.png",
        "alt": "An open Mimosa pudica leaf and the same leaf closed after touch, with pinnae labelled.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 8
        }
      }
    ],
    "parts": [
      {
        "key": "a",
        "label": "(a)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 8,
          "ms": 9
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A touch stimulus to an <i>M. pudica</i> leaf causes an action potential to be generated.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 8
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The action potential results in changes in cells, which cause the leaf to close.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 8
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fig. 4.3 shows the mechanism in <i>M. pudica</i> cells that causes the leaf to close.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 8
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "figure",
            "id": "fig4.3",
            "caption": "<b>Fig. 4.3</b>",
            "src": "figures/q4_fig4_3.png",
            "alt": "Sequence from an action potential after touch to proton pumping, ion loss, osmosis and flaccidity in Mimosa pudica cells.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 8
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The leaves of <i>M. pudica</i> and the leaves of Venus fly traps move in response to touch stimuli, but the mechanisms that cause the responses are different.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 8
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Describe the differences between the mechanism shown in Fig. 4.3 and the mechanism that causes the closure of the modified leaves in Venus fly traps.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 8
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 3,
